{"org":"elastic","repo":"elasticsearch","prNumber":"118454","commit":"dc5840e560b8999ec7ae8e951e3d4a69b0847e2a","generatedCommentsNodes":[{"comment":"`RegexMatch#fold()` now throws `UnsupportedOperationException` instead of performing the fold. This is a deliberate design choice: the base class signals \"you must override,\" and the concrete classes do. However, note that the previous implementation had a latent bug — it called the 2-arg `RegexOperation.match(val, pattern)` which is always case-sensitive, ignoring the `caseInsensitive()` flag. The new delegation to the evaluator presumably fixes this (consistent with the `FIXED_REGEX_FOLD` capability in `#V6M0E`). No issue with the change itself.","nodes":[{"id":"x-pack/plugin/esql-core/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/esql/core/expression/predicate/regex/RegexMatch.java-x-pack/plugin/esql-core/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/esql/core/expression/predicate/regex/RegexMatch.java-2030:2058,2067:2147,2156:2214-1990:2032","promptId":"#R1YHA","srcPath":"x-pack/plugin/esql-core/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/esql/core/expression/predicate/regex/RegexMatch.java","dstPath":"x-pack/plugin/esql-core/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/esql/core/expression/predicate/regex/RegexMatch.java","srcChanges":[{"pos":2030,"endPos":2058,"startLine":67,"startLineOffset":8,"endLine":67,"endLineOffset":36,"length":29},{"pos":2067,"endPos":2147,"startLine":68,"startLineOffset":8,"endLine":70,"endLineOffset":9,"length":81},{"pos":2156,"endPos":2214,"startLine":71,"startLineOffset":8,"endLine":71,"endLineOffset":66,"length":59}],"dstChanges":[{"pos":1990,"endPos":2032,"startLine":66,"startLineOffset":8,"endLine":66,"endLineOffset":50,"length":43}]}]},{"comment":"The `TestUtils.rlike()` helper is removed. `#56BVC` updates one call-site, but please verify that no other test classes still import or call `TestUtils.rlike(...)`, as this will cause a compile failure.","nodes":[{"id":"x-pack/plugin/esql-core/src/test/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/esql/core/util/TestUtils.java-x-pack/plugin/esql-core/src/test/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/esql/core/util/TestUtils.java-2047:2171-","promptId":"#NP9D1","srcPath":"x-pack/plugin/esql-core/src/test/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/esql/core/util/TestUtils.java","dstPath":"x-pack/plugin/esql-core/src/test/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/esql/core/util/TestUtils.java","srcChanges":[{"pos":2047,"endPos":2171,"startLine":49,"startLineOffset":4,"endLine":51,"endLineOffset":5,"length":125}],"dstChanges":[]}]},{"comment":"Making the core classes abstract is a clean way to force concrete subclasses to supply `info()` and `fold()`. No issue.","nodes":[{"id":"x-pack/plugin/esql-core/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/esql/core/expression/predicate/regex/RLike.java-x-pack/plugin/esql-core/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/esql/core/expression/predicate/regex/RLike.java--535:543","promptId":"#E9N4K","srcPath":"x-pack/plugin/esql-core/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/esql/core/expression/predicate/regex/RLike.java","dstPath":"x-pack/plugin/esql-core/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/esql/core/expression/predicate/regex/RLike.java","srcChanges":[],"dstChanges":[{"pos":535,"endPos":543,"startLine":15,"startLineOffset":7,"endLine":15,"endLineOffset":15,"length":9}]},{"id":"x-pack/plugin/esql-core/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/esql/core/expression/predicate/regex/WildcardLike.java-x-pack/plugin/esql-core/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/esql/core/expression/predicate/regex/WildcardLike.java--535:543","promptId":"#AWZ4H","srcPath":"x-pack/plugin/esql-core/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/esql/core/expression/predicate/regex/WildcardLike.java","dstPath":"x-pack/plugin/esql-core/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/esql/core/expression/predicate/regex/WildcardLike.java","srcChanges":[],"dstChanges":[{"pos":535,"endPos":543,"startLine":15,"startLineOffset":7,"endLine":15,"endLineOffset":15,"length":9}]}]},{"comment":"Both concrete classes add an identical `fold()` that delegates to `EvaluatorMapper.super.fold()`. Looking at the `EvaluatorMapper.fold()` default implementation (provided as a dependency), it creates an evaluator whose `eval` method calls `e.fold()`, where `e` is the expression supplied by `toEvaluator`. If `toEvaluator` supplies the same `RLike`/`WildcardLike` instance, the call chain becomes:\n`RLike.fold()` → `EvaluatorMapper.super.fold()` → evaluator.eval() → `e.fold()` → `RLike.fold()` → …\nThis would be infinite recursion. The Javadoc on `EvaluatorMapper.fold()` says the evaluator \"will always call `Expression#fold`,\" which reinforces this concern. Please verify that `toEvaluator` does not feed the same expression back into the lambda, or that there is a guard preventing the re-entrant call. If `e` is guaranteed to be a *child* expression (e.g., the field), the recursion risk is gone, but the evaluator\u0027s `eval` would then only return the folded field value rather than the regex-match `Boolean`, which would be a type/logic mismatch with the `(Boolean)` cast.","nodes":[{"id":"x-pack/plugin/esql/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/esql/expression/function/scalar/string/WildcardLike.java-x-pack/plugin/esql/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/esql/expression/function/scalar/string/WildcardLike.java--4219:4317","promptId":"#8WBBE","srcPath":"x-pack/plugin/esql/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/esql/expression/function/scalar/string/WildcardLike.java","dstPath":"x-pack/plugin/esql/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/esql/expression/function/scalar/string/WildcardLike.java","srcChanges":[],"dstChanges":[{"pos":4219,"endPos":4317,"startLine":102,"startLineOffset":4,"endLine":105,"endLineOffset":5,"length":99}]},{"id":"x-pack/plugin/esql/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/esql/expression/function/scalar/string/RLike.java-x-pack/plugin/esql/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/esql/expression/function/scalar/string/RLike.java--4123:4221","promptId":"#NK6R7","srcPath":"x-pac